  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Safeguarding Personal Data in Indonesia&#x2019;s Criminal Justice System</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project, led by Jentera Indonesia School of Law and supported by the UK Ministry of Justice, aims to strengthen personal data protection within Indonesia&#x2019;s criminal justice system. It responds to growing risks posed by digital transformation and the limited implementation of the Personal Data Protection Law (Law No. 27/2022). The initiative will assess legal and institutional frameworks, map data flows, and identify gaps in current practices. It will also build capacity through scenario-based pilot training involving investigators, prosecutors, judges, and IT officers, co-facilitated by UK and Indonesian experts. Outputs include a baseline report, policy recommendations, training materials, and a facilitation guide. The project emphasizes ethical data governance, especially for vulnerable groups such as children and survivors of gender-based violence. Sustainability is ensured through institutional engagement and integration of training modules into formal systems. Gender sensitivity, climate responsibility, and human rights safeguards are embedded throughout. The project runs from October 2025 to March 2026 and aims to institutionalize secure data practices, reduce privacy harms, and enhance public trust in justice institutions.</narrative>
  </description>
